nalysis, allocation reduction, benchmarks.",[89,720,721,724,730],{},[107,722,723],{},"28",[107,725,726],{},[113,727,729],{"href":728},"\u002Fgo\u002F28-exercises-and-projects","Exercises & Project Ideas",[107,731,732],{},"From beginner to pro.",[19,734,736],{"id":735},"learning-path-suggestions","Learning Path Suggestions",[78,738,740],{"id":739},"if-youre-new-to-programming","If you're new to programming",[24,742,743,746,749,755],{},[27,744,745],{},"Read 01–06 in order.",[27,747,748],{},"Build small programs with slices and maps (07–08).",[27,750,751,752,754],{},"Read 15 (error handling) — ",[63,753,427],{}," is the Go culture.",[27,756,757],{},"Do exercises 1–5 in chapter 28.",[78,759,761],{"id":760},"if-youre-coming-from-pythonrubyjs","If you're coming from Python\u002FRuby\u002FJS",[15,763,764],{},"Go's static typing and explicit error handling are the big shifts. Read 03–05 (types, functions), 15 (errors — no exceptions), and 16–20 (concurrency — goroutines\u002Fchannels are unique to Go). Don't skip 07 (slices — they're not Python lists).",[78,766,768],{"id":767},"if-youre-coming-from-ccrust","If you're coming from C\u002FC++\u002FRust",[15,770,771,772,775],{},"Go is simpler but has surprises: garbage collection (no manual memory management), goroutines (not OS threads), channels (not mutexes as the default), and implicit interfaces (no ",[63,773,774],{},"implements","). Read 12 (interfaces), 16–18 (concurrency), 14 (generics — simpler than C++ templates).",[78,777,779],{"id":778},"if-youre-a-senior-engineer","If you're a senior engineer",[15,781,782],{},"Skim 01–11. Read 12 (interfaces), 14 (generics), 15 (errors), 16–20 (concurrency — Go's defining feature), 20 (context), 26 (patterns), 27 (profiling) closely. Use 28 to pick a project.",[19,784,786],{"id":785},"companion-resources","Companion Resources",[57,788,789,798,806,814,822,830],{},[27,790,791,797],{},[113,792,796],{"href":793,"rel":794},"https:\u002F\u002Fgo.dev\u002Ftour",[795],"nofollow","A Tour of